                 argues that scaling model capacity is insufficient to solve the
                 Virtual Cell problem because the primary failure mode is a lack
                 of adequate coverage over diverse biological contexts, not
                 insufficient model expressivity. We support this claim by
                 reviewing recent studies showing that simple baselines perform
                 on par with sophisticated architectures within a given
                 biological context, and current models fail to consistently
                 generalize across contexts. We connect this finding to the
                 causal inference literature on transportability and contrast it
                 with domains where scaling has succeeded. We substantiate our
                 argument through analysis of a state-of-the-art model on a
